WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.800
Popcorn costing under $0.50 sells for $8 inside most movie theaters. This is First Principles Daily, Episode 152. The ticket split that pushed exhibitors onto concessions, the atoms in a bucket and a cup, the contracts that multiply those atoms, and the few moves that would let a lower price be tested. A medium bucket that theaters buy and prepare for roughly $0.40 to $0.90 reaches the customer at $0.79. Soda follows the same shape.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:33.600
syrup, cup, lid, straw, and carbonated water together run under a dollar, and the drink

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.700
sells for five to six dollars. Those spreads are not hidden. They sit on every concession

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:43.840
menu and in the public filings of the largest chains. The gap between raw inputs and the

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.440
price on the board is the place a first principles look at the concession model has to begin.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:54.940
That gap is not an accident of ingredients. It is the result of a revenue split set in 1948.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:01:00.780
Movie theaters receive only about 40% of each ticket dollar on average after studio splits,

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.020
and the share can drop below 30% in the opening weeks of major releases.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.460
That split traces directly to the 1948 Paramount Decree, which forced studios to divest their

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.460
theater holdings and ended block booking.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.680
With most ticket revenue leaving the building, operators turn to concessions as the one stream

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:19.520
they keep in full.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.960
Once the ticket is bought, the customer has no competing outlet inside the venue.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.280
So the theater holds a local monopoly on food and drink.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.940
Chain set prices to cover building overhead,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.220
staff wages, utilities, and rent.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.860
Not to track the commodity underneath the menu.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.360
Public filing show concession supplies

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.560
running between 18 and 19% of concession revenue

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.980
for AMC and Cinemark in 2025,

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.180
which leaves roughly 80% gross margin before labor and space.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.740
Independent operators report similar or higher figures,

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:55.220
with blended concession cost of goods sold between 20% and 30%.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:02:00.160
Industry benchmarks put popcorn kernels and flavored oil together below 50 cents for

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:00.920
a large serving.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:07.500
Soda syrup, packaging and carbonation add roughly 40-90 cents, depending on cup size and

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.900
whether a refill is included.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.980
Those input costs are well known inside the trade.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.620
They still put little downward pressure on the board, because a captive audience removes

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.880
the retail discipline that would otherwise punish a wide markup.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:25.740
What remains is a stable equilibrium in which the markup feels normal to the operator and to the customer.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.900
One objection is that theaters could simply raise ticket prices and lean less on the stand.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:36.280
Contractual film rental formulas often tie the studio's percentage to the ticket price itself.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.660
And across the board increase would send a larger absolute amount out the door

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.560
and still leave the theater with the same fractional share.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:48.000
Another objection is that labor and real estate have to be recovered somewhere.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.720
Those costs are real, they are also incurred whether the stand sells one bucket or 100.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:59.240
The pricing architecture spreads them across a narrow set of high margin items rather than

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:01.160
across every seat in the auditorium.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:06.000
The 1948 decree did not create the captive audience condition by itself.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.980
It removed the studio's ability to own the outlets, and with that ownership it removed

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.940
any incentive for the studios to keep concession prices low in order to sell more tickets.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:16.120
units.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:21.420
Once ownership and exhibition were separated, the remaining exhibitors faced a revenue mix

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.360
in which concessions were the only line not subject to percentage remittance.

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:31.120
That structural fact, not any particular ingredient, is what keeps the current pricing model intact.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:36.740
With that structure in place, the real question is how far the final price sits from the atoms.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:42.700
Wave a magic wand and arrange the raw atoms into finished popcorn and soda for free, and

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:49.480
And the floor is only the commodities, unpopped kernels, coconut or soybean oil, salt, butter-flavored

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:56.540
topping, corn syrup concentrate, carbon dioxide, paper or plastic cups, lids and straws.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:02.020
A rough tally for a large popcorn starts with 20 to 30 cents of kernels in oil, another

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:06.540
10 to 15 cents for seasoning and topping, and perhaps 5 cents for the bucket.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:11.860
Add those pieces and the combined raw material estimate lands at 40 to 60 cents.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:18.660
For a large soda, the syrup runs 20-40 cents, the cup and lid another 7-10 cents, the

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:22.580
straw 1-2 cents, and the carbonated water under 5 cents.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.960
Put a refill allowance on top, and the total sits near 50-60 cents.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:32.200
These are rough commodity figures drawn from supplier catalogs and published research summaries.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:36.560
Actual delivered costs vary with volume contracts and regional pricing.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:41.820
Now, the idiot index, which is just the finished price, divided by the materials inside it.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:45.840
Take an $8 popcorn price and divide by a 60 cent floor.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.260
Eight divided by 6 tenths is a bit over 13.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.540
Divide that same $8 by a 40 cent floor and you get 20.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.540
So the implied index sits between roughly 13 and 20.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.120
Do the same for soda.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:02.020
$6 divided by 60 cents is 10.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:08.680
against a $6 soda the index lands between 10 and 12. A low index would mean the price is already

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:15.040
near the physical limit. 13 to 20 means most of what the customer pays is not kernels, oil, syrup

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:21.700
or paper. The gap does not reside in the kernels or the syrup. It accumulates in three places in order.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:28.440
First, exclusive pouring rights contracts lock out lower cost suppliers. Second, a single stand has

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:33.920
to absorb all the labor, cleaning, and equipment depreciation for a few hours of peak use each day.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:38.460
Third, there is no on-site competitor that would otherwise compress the margin.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.040
Packaging and portion control systems add further layers,

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:47.100
because theaters buy pre-printed branded cups size to support the price architecture

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:52.800
rather than the lowest-cost generic container. The same logic applies to the butter-flavored oil

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:58.140
dispensers and the poppers, which are size for throughput rather than for minimal material handling.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:03.560
Each of those steps is rational under the current revenue split and the captive audience constraint.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:07.540
Each one also multiplies the distance from the raw material floor.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:13.200
A common pushback is that volume purchasing already drives the inputs close to the commodity floor.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.160
Even if that is true for the kernels and the syrup,

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:22.600
the custom printed buckets and the exclusive contract overhead sit outside any volume discount on raw corn.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:27.560
Another objection is that the stand must be staffed and cleaned regardless of volume.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:32.300
them. Those fixed costs are incurred. They are the same whether the menu is marked up

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:37.760
10 times or 3 times. The high markup simply makes the same labor hours appear profitable.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:43.240
The idiot index isolates the portion of the final price that atoms and ordinary operating

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:48.340
overhead cannot explain, and it points instead at the contractual and locational features

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:53.920
that prevent price discovery. If the gap is contractual rather than material, a redesign

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:58.980
has to touch the contract. A redesign would start by separating commodity delivery

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:04.260
from the venue monopoly. One path is to allow third-party vendors inside the lobby under

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:09.840
regulated space leases, which would introduce price discovery on the same inputs. Another

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.980
is to move high-margin items to prepackaged, shelf-stable formats that could be sourced

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:20.220
at scale from grocery suppliers and sold with minimal on-site handling. Either change

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:24.820
would require renegotiating the exclusive beverage contracts that currently tie chains

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:30.540
to single-sirit providers. A further step would standardize cup and bucket sizes across chains,

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:35.400
so packaging could be bid as a commodity instead of ordered as a custom printing run.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.960
None of those moves eliminates the need to cover theater overhead. They expose which costs are

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:46.580
truly irreducible and which costs are an artifact of how the stand is locked. The hardest constraint

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:51.540
remains the paramount error revenue split on tickets, which still pushes operators toward

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:57.060
concessions as the primary profit center. Policy changes that altered film rental terms,

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:02.320
or that permitted broader food service competition, would therefore have larger effects than any

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:08.280
single operational tweak. Until those conditions shift, the high idiot index functions less as an

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:13.240
engineering problem and more as a contractual and regulatory feature of the exhibition business.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:20.120
One objection is that third-party vendors would still pay rent to the theater and would pass some of that cost to customers.

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:26.960
The difference is that the rent would be a negotiated lease rather than an implicit monopoly surcharge baked into every cup.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:32.840
Another objection is that pre-packaged items might reduce the theater experience some patrons' value.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:38.020
That preference is real. It could be tested by offering both options side by side.

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:40.680
The current model prevents the test from occurring.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:45.720
The sequence that would matter most is therefore first the loosening of exclusive contracts,

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:50.800
second the standardization of packaging sizes, and third the introduction of competing

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:53.220
points of sale inside the same four walls.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:57.300
Any such change would test a deeper principle about access pricing.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:02.200
When a venue can charge for access and then charge again for every item consumed inside

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:07.160
that access, the second price is set by the first constraint, not by material cost.

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:11.520
The ticket is the first constraint, it gets the customer through the door, and

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:16.600
after 1948, most of that ticket dollar does not stay with the exhibitor. The stand is

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:21.860
the second price, kernels, oil, syrup, and a cup can sit near half a dollar or a little

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:28.300
above it, and the menu can still sit at 5, 6, 7, 8, or 9 dollars, because the customer who

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:37.140
has already paid for the seat has nowhere else in the building to buy the same atoms. The

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:42.700
soda is the numerical trace of that sequence. It is not a verdict on corn. It is a measure

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:48.040
of how far a locked point of sale can travel from the commodity floor when the usual comparison

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:53.720
shopper has been left outside. A second principle follows from the same structure. Any product

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:58.540
whose largest expense line is the right to sell it, rather than the atoms inside it, will

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:04.000
resist ordinary cost reduction pressure until that right is renegotiated. Volume discounts

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.860
accounts on kernels do not touch a pouring rights contract.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:09.220
A cheaper generic cup does not appear

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:11.480
while the cup has to be a custom printed piece

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:12.680
of the price architecture.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:14.940
Staffing the stand more efficiently

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:17.020
does not create a second vendor in the lobby.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:19.440
The labor hours and the rent are real,

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:22.400
and they have to be paid, but they do not explain

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.300
why the same hours look profitable

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.740
only when the markup is 10 times the materials instead of three.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.680
Until the right to be the only seller is opened,

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:32.440
bid or leased out in the open,

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:37.980
cost-cutting at the atom stays a small number next to a large one. That is why the operational

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:41.240
moves and the policy constraint are not the same size of prize.

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:47.400
Loosening exclusive syrup contracts, standardizing cup and bucket sizes, and letting a third

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:52.340
party sell inside the lobby would each pull some distance out of the index, and a side-by-side

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:57.100
offer would show whether patrons actually want the fresh stand, the packaged alternative,

00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:02.240
or both. The ticket split would still be there. As long as the studio share is tied to

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:07.020
the ticket price, raising the ticket to take pressure off the stand sends more absolute

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:10.480
dollars out of the building and leaves the exhibitor with the same fraction.

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:15.060
The concession line remains the only line not subject to percentage remittance.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:18.580
A lower idiot index inside that structure is possible.

00:11:19.595 --> 00:11:24.855
A low one close to the physical limit waits on a change to the right to sell, not on a cheaper

00:11:24.855 --> 00:11:25.255
kernel.

00:11:25.735 --> 00:11:28.835
Who might test a lower idiot index concession model first?

00:11:28.835 --> 00:11:33.555
and what measurable change in per-patron spend would signal that the test is working.

00:11:34.115 --> 00:11:36.775
That's a wrap on today's First Principles Breakdown.

00:11:37.315 --> 00:11:39.555
The ideas travel further when you pass them on.

